// @trap
// @hint redundant type annotation: `ff64` is inferred as `f64`
// An `x as! u8` TRAPS on a fraction as well as on a range miss: the test is "integral AND in
// range", the same two questions `as! i32` asks, so `3.9 as! u8` is refused for the reason
// `3.9 as! i32` is even though 3 would fit a byte comfortably (D1587).
//
// `trunc(d) as! u8` skips only the integrality half; the range half is the byte's, since
// `i32.trunc_f64_s` enforces i32's range and not 0..255 (`as-cast-integral-operand-trap-u8.vl`).
function ff64(x: f64): f64 { x }
const frac = ff64(3.9)
print(frac as! u8)
print(-1)
